{"cases": [{"attack": false, "case": "", "counterexample": null, "target_alias": "SRC_001"}, {"attack": true, "case": "SRC_002 conflates write-atomicity with resumption safety. Its causal mechanism only ensures that no write is interrupted, then leaps to 'resumption is always safe provided the reservation protocol was followed.' But clean event boundaries do not guarantee the system can produce coherent continuation events: non-deterministic state consumed during reasoning (sampling, tool responses, external API outputs) may have influenced which event was generated next, and if that state is not captured in the event log, resumed events will follow a different trajectory even though every individual write was atomic. The problem asks for conditions under which resumption does not corrupt the append-only IDENTITY of recorded events — not merely their structural integrity. A resumed run that appends structurally valid but trajectory-incoherent events corrupts the identity of the event sequence as a single coherent run. SRC_002's refusal cases address only protocol violations and estimate errors, never the case where the reservation protocol was perfectly followed but non-deterministic reasoning state was lost. Furthermore, the entire answer is conditional on one specific design (per-event reservation), providing no conditions for systems that do not use that protocol, which the problem does not assume.", "counterexample": null, "target_alias": "SRC_002"}, {"attack": false, "case": "", "counterexample": null, "target_alias": "SRC_003"}, {"attack": true, "case": "SRC_004's structural resumption condition is stated as an iff: 'A halted run can be resumed without structural corruption iff the tail event is fully committed with a finalized digest (clean halt).' This condition is necessary but not sufficient. A finalized tail digest only confirms the last write completed; it does not verify that earlier entries in the hash chain are intact. If a prior crash (not the current halt) left a torn write in the middle of the chain, the tail's digest could still be finalized and link to its predecessor, yet the chain as a whole would be broken. Resuming from such a log would append events to a structurally corrupted prefix, violating append-only identity. SRC_001 and SRC_003 both require the chain to be 'self-verifying up to its last entry,' which is strictly stronger than SRC_004's tail-only check. The iff therefore overstates sufficiency: full-chain verification is also required, and SRC_004's structural branch omits it.", "counterexample": null, "target_alias": "SRC_004"}]}